{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And as it was budding, its blossom shot up</b> — This is similar to פּוֹרַחַת: וְהִיא כְּפוֹרַחַת, 'it seemed to me in my dream as though it was budding,' and after the budding, עָלְתָה נִצָּהּ, 'its blossom shot up' — it produced tendrils, and afterward, הִבְשִׁילוּ, 'they ripened.' וְהִיא כַּד אַפְרַחַת אַפֵּקַת לַבְלַבִּין — thus far is the translation of פּוֹרַחַת; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 40:10). But it is not correct, for if he were speaking in a comparative mode because these are words of a dream, he would have said וְהִנֵּה כְגֶפֶן לְפָנַי וּבַגֶּפֶן כִּשְׁלֹשָׁה שָׂרִיגִים — yet this כ ('as/if') appears neither in the dream of the chief baker nor in the dream of Pharaoh; so why would he use a comparison here more than in all the others? Rather, he says וְהִנֵּה in all of them, and that itself is the comparison, its meaning being 'as if.' But the meaning of וְהִיא כְפֹרַחַת עָלְתָה נִצָּהּ is that in his dream he saw the vine that as soon as it budded, its blossom shot up and its clusters ripened into grapes — this was to indicate that Elohim was hastening to accomplish it. And by this Joseph recognized that the three branches were three days and not months or years, and he reasoned that on a single day the heads of both of them would be lifted, or because they dreamed on a single night. And there is no need for the words of Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra], who said that Joseph knew the day of Pharaoh's birthday. And this כ ('as') in this sense appears in many places: וַיְהִי כְּמֵשִׁיב יָדוֹ (Genesis 38:29), כְּבֹא אַבְרָם (Genesis 12:14), וּכְעֵת מוּתָהּ וַתְּדַבֵּרְנָה הַנִּצָּבוֹת (1 Samuel 4:20), and many others. And Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 40:10), who said וְהִיא כַּד אַפְרַחַת אַפֵּקַת לַבְלַבִּין, translated עָלְתָה — meaning 'it brought up vine tendrils': it produced a flower and brought forth large shoots (לוּלָבִין), and they blossomed and its clusters ripened into grapes — for the term עָלְתָה would not apply to a blossom (נֵץ), since a blossom does not 'go up.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {0 "Where Ramban first quotes Rashi and then paraphrases the Onkelos Aramaic phrase וְהִיא כַּד אַפְרַחַת אַפֵּקַת לַבְלַבִּין, I left the Aramaic in Hebrew script as Ramban quotes it, per the rule that Hebrew forms he quotes stay in Hebrew script. In the final sentence, לוּלָבִין in context refers to vine shoots/tendrils (not palm branches); rendered literally as 'shoots' to preserve the botanical argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:42:37.168538495Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}